Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:01.670 align:start position:0%
 
in the wake of the police killings of  george floyd and brianna taylor last  year tarana burke founder of the metoo  movement and new york times best-selling  author brene brown  think of those two together teamed up to  co-ed and co-edit a new book it's called  you  are your best thing love that title  vulnerability shame resilience  and the black experience the collection  of essays from black men  and women is about their lives in  overcoming racism  writer austin channing brown is one of  the voices in the book  her essay this joy i have reflects on  heartache and hope  she read some excerpts from her piece  just for us  [Music]  racism is the silent stalker always  willing  to ring joy from our lives this level of  foreboding joy is not in our heads  it's in the evidence of our experience  when my husband is in the car i do not  fear only the possibility of an accident  but the possibility of a police officer  harassing him  maybe even taking his life  we have learned  that the only thing white supremacy  would love more than taking our lives  is for the lives we have to be  diminished  less than human filled with despair  containing only fear the joy of  blackness  persists our joy is in the legacy  of all that our ancestors have done for  us  our joy is being able to participate in  that legacy now  our joy is in having left a mark in the  world  being able to say i was here  [Music]  when i look into my little boy's eyes  and wonder if his life will mirror  trayvon's  i silently thank trayvon martin for his  life  i will love him harder and in this  become  softer i will be vulnerable  open to being hurt because i trust that  my joy in him  cannot be taken away  beautiful picture of her with her little  baby boy tarana burke and brene brown  join us now toronto we should say  also recently signed a production deal  with cbs studios  i didn't know that toronto so i'll just  say welcome to the family  and it's good to see you both this  morning those essays guys are so  powerful you know i pulled out a couple  laverne cox who says  she never leaves home without her armor  up i like that  professor mark lamont hill says therapy  is a staple of his emotional diet  in the beginning it was a badge of shame  and now it's key to survival  i mean you just have one powerful  reading after another after another so  tarana i want to start with you  what were you trying to accomplish with  the essays what were you looking for and  why did you think renee brown would be  the perfect partner for this  well brene and i are friends and i had  many discussions with her about some of  these topics  and when this came up when george floyd  and brianna taylor hit us like a ton of  bricks last summer  i was really sad and i watched a lot of  black people around me  who were also sad and felt a range of  emotions but nobody really were tapping  into  what we were feeling and as much as they  were asking how can we be better people  how can we be anti-racist and things  like that  and so i wanted to talk to brene about  you know how her work had helped me but  also about how  we can contribute to this moment and you  know  true to form she was very very very much  down with it as soon as i asked the  question were you reluctant to ask her  i was very reluctant why i mean i didn't  know our friendship had  been well because our friendship had  just been a friendship right we didn't  really talk about work and things of  that nature  and i was i was saying to her i think  your work is important for black people  but i also think that  i can't really find myself in your work  right away and i want to i want to  change that  and that's a big thing to say to  somebody about their body of work and  also it's a test of a friendship  so renee tarana just said you were all  in were you hesitant at first yourself  no this is something i've been grappling  with for a really long time  and because i had always had  incredibly diverse samples i knew that  what needed to change was the telling of  the stories that  brought the data to life i knew the data  were representative of large groups of  people but i use a lot of my own stories  and i thought  how i don't want people to have to work  hard to find themselves  in the work and so when she teed up the  idea  of a collaboration i think my answer was  a heck yes  or some version of that yeah but i  understand at one point you said should  a white person be talking about this  how do we navigate that is that our  conversation  yes because i was like i said look you  can have the data you i can support it i  can help but do you  do we want to code do you want me to  co-edit this with you and she's like  yeah you're you're the expert in this  area and i said  i i'm white and she's like yeah i'm  clear  i get it yeah  but i thought maybe we can do this in a  way that really models what co-creation  should be around um and as long as there  were some stipulations that  lived experience has to always trump  academic experience  she needed to take lead authorship yeah  i did think that was interesting  incredible project i did think it was  interesting when you say you know  uh life experience always trumps  academics and tarana i've heard you say  that vulnerability  is a word we all know but it's a  different experience when you talk to  black people  than when you talk to white people what  do you mean  absolutely you know brene talks about a  story in the book where she's  feeling vulnerable because she's in the  store with her daughter and her  daughter's being silly  and you know when we compare that to  what my experience could be  in the store with my daughter and i  could be stopped for shoplifting or i  feel vulnerable  in a different way because of my race  and because of how people perceive me  because of my race  and so when you talk to me about being  vulnerability as a pathway to a  wholehearted life  that feels scary to me because i don't  know what vulnerability  how that's going to affect me you know  in the world  it's a very different experience for for  black people and brene i've heard you  say that racism is trauma  what do you mean by that and how does  that connect to vulnerability  yeah i think one of the greatest  casualties of trauma  is trauma pulls from us  the ability willingness the space to be  vulnerable i mean you see this um you  know  if i see this when i work with vets i  see this when i work with people who've  been through violent crimes like you're  wanting to me  you want me to take the armor off i've  seen what that looks like but  i think what we don't understand is that  racism  persistent dehumanization that's trauma  as well  and so it's really important to  understand  that safety emotional safety  physical safety are prerequisites  for vulnerability and we all have a  responsibility to create  that environment because just because  you're black or you've gone through  trauma doesn't  mean that you need less vulnerability in  your life  um to know intimacy and trust and  creativity and innovation  but it means it's harder to come by  because we don't have  the safety what i thought that you both  captured so beautifully in the book  is about humanity it's not a story we  often hear about black and brown people  one of the strongest essays is from you  tarana burke we're out of time so people  have to get the book  where you talk about for the first time  having a stroke  it's very very powerful and i'm glad  you're okay glad you're okay  tarana burke and brene brown we thank  you both you are your best thing goes on  sale tomorrow  wherever you like to buy your books  you're watching cbs this morning

